// A page of staff notation, read by an adapter and owned by a package.
//
// `syntax staff { … }` is not ordinary Musa. The region's contents are read by
// the fixed lexer and grouper and handed to `std::adapters::staff`, which
// answers with one ordinary expression — a call to `std::notation::staff`'s
// `Document` constructor — that is then resolved, checked, and evaluated as if
// it had been written out here. The adapter reads; the package decides.
//
// Everything a musician states on a page is stated here: the instrument, how
// far its written pitch sits from its sounding pitch, the clef, the key, the
// time it opens in, and how written values are to be spelled. Then the notation itself —
// a pickup, a repeat with two endings, bars that each say what they measure,
// notes, rests, chords, dots, exact durations, a tie across a bar line, a
// slur, a tuplet, a grace note, and a meter change.
//
// Nothing is inherited. Every note states its own register and its own written
// value, and every bar states its own length, so any one of them can be read
// without reading the one before it — which is also what lets the adapter
// report an unfull bar against the bar the composer wrote rather than against
// the page.
//
// # Why the score below is written out rather than realized from the page
//
// The page holds a grace note, and a grace note has no written span: when it
// sounds is a performance question, and `std::notation::staff` refuses to
// invent an answer to it. So `realize` refuses this page rather than placing
// its grace somewhere, which is the deliberate silence rather than a fault.
// A staff page becomes sound when a performance profile says how it is played;
// until then it is notation, and the score below is the page's two repeated
// bars written in the language the compiler reads today — the tie written as
// its two halves, and `c5(3/8)` as the dotted quarter it is worth.
//
// The head writes `time 4/4` and the bars write `bar (4, 4)`, which is one
// signature spelled the two ways the adapter admits. A rational reaches an
// adapter as the parts the lexer found, so `4/4` is a count of a unit here and
// not the whole note it reduces to; `(4, 4)` says the same thing in the form
// every other numbered word on the page uses.

piece "Staff page" {
    import std::notation::staff;
    import syntax std::adapters::staff as staff;

    let page: StaffDocument = syntax staff {
        instrument "bb_clarinet"
        transposing M2
        clef treble
        key d major
        time 4/4
        spelling shortest_readable
        pickup (1, 4) { g4/4 }
        repeat (2) {
            bar (4, 4) { c5/4 d5/4. e5/8 f5/4 ~ }
            bar (4, 4) { f5/8 rest/8 [g4 b4 d5]/4 c5(3/8) rest/8 }
            ending (1) {
                bar (4, 4) { slur { tuplet (3, 2) { a4/8 b4/8 c5/8 } d5/4 } grace [e5] f5/2 }
            }
            ending (2) {
                meter (3, 4)
                bar (3, 4) { g5/4 a5/4 b5/4 }
            }
        }
    };

    tempo 1/4 = 96;
    meter 4/4;
    key d major;

    score {
        part clarinet {
            voice line {
                | c5/4  d5/4. e5/8  f5/4
                | f5/8 rest/8  [g4 b4 d5]/4  c5/4. rest/8
            }
        }
    }
}
